Markdown, the owner's Markdown viewer and editor, the third of the owner's programs on Zenix (roadmap block 3: the
text-UI library, then Sumi, Maki, Markdown; DESIGN D27 section 9; docs/d27-c-library.md, "Maki and Markdown on
Zenix"; the user's page is docs/man/markdown.txt)

What it is: a viewer that shows Markdown files the way they read (headings, emphasis, lists, quotes, code blocks with
colours, tables, links, an outline) and edits them with Sumi's editor, in the terminal, written for AmigaOS over
tui.library. On Zenix it is /bin/markdown, a program with shared code (ZEX2: a second one costs only its data),
linked with the text-UI library's pure build (build/libtui-pure.a, ports/tui) and the C library's (build/libc-pure.a).

upstream/  the owner's source, one source for AmigaOS and Zenix (D27 9, rule 1; owner 2026-10-28), pinned here and
           never changed: the owner's TextTools repository (public, MIT), commit
           533439214cf4225feed4f0e748c3b3f20099e60b (2026-10-22), Markdown 0.1b5: src/*.c and src/*.h, README.md as
           README (the user's documentation, which docs/man/markdown.txt follows) and LICENSE (MIT, the notice that
           travels with the program: tools/mkroot.sh and the package markdown put it in /usr/share/licenses/markdown,
           with the text-UI library's as markdown-tui).

Zenix's layer is ports/common (GPL-2.0-only, shared with Sumi and Maki): AmigaOS's names over the C library; the
name Markdown maps is "Markdown" (ENVARC:Markdown, ENV:Markdown, T:Markdown-). What each call does and the choices
(names, protection bits, "run" through sh, the clipboard) are in ports/common/README; Markdown's call into
openurl.library (an address followed: to the browser) is there as inline/macros.h's LP2, never reached (no library
opens), so an address is copied inside the program instead, upstream's own way without OpenURL.

Markdown's own (for the main session to confirm with the layer's choices):
- The stack: 32 KB (STACK_markdown), upstream's own (its STACKSZ, the stack it swaps to on AmigaOS): the renderer
  (md.c) recurses once per container inside a container, about 1 KB a level (blocks 984 bytes, -fstack-usage), up to
  MAXDEPTH 24; tools/markdown-test.sh renders 20. Upstream's md.c can be built with MAXDEPTH 10 (as its
  markdown.datatype is, on a 16 KB stack): 16 KB less for each Markdown running, nesting cut at 10; not done (the
  same program on both systems first).
- Pictures: none (the console has no cells of its own size, tui_cellw 0): upstream then names them in a line.

Found in upstream (for the owner's repository, not changed here):
- Memory: about 241 KB of data while one file is open on 80 x 32 (ps), of which the stack is 32 KB and the text-UI
  library's two grids 80 KB (16-byte cells), and 131 KB of shared code (the largest of the three programs; the editor
  is Sumi's code a second time, compiled into this program).
- The editor half is a copy of Sumi's files (buf.c, file.c, syntax.c, clip.c, keys.c; the same names, changed apart
  since): two copies in one repository to keep in step; a shared source for the two programs would make it one.
- A link's target is made an AmigaDOS path (md_resolve, md.c): "../x" becomes "/x" after the file's folder, which on
  AmigaOS is the parent; on Zenix "dir//x" is "dir/x" and, beside a file named without a folder, "/x" the root: a
  link that goes up a folder opens the wrong file (read from the source, not tested). The OS layer joining the parts
  (AmigaOS's AddPart, a Unix join here) would make it right on both.
- Messages and help keep AmigaOS words ("Keys: ENVARC:Markdown/Markdown.keys", "an AmigaDOS command"), as Sumi's do.

Tests: tools/markdown-test.sh (FS-UAE, the stock A1200; in make check): markdown opens a .md file, the view is read
back with termtest screen (CON_READ): headings, emphasis, lists, a code block, quotes, 20 quotes inside each other,
the status line; e (CON_INPUT) turns it into the source to edit, read back too; the settings; the memory printed.

To update: tools/texttools-vendor.sh markdown /path/to/TextTools [COMMIT] (the files above from that commit,
unchanged), put the commit here, build, run tools/markdown-test.sh, and bring docs/man/markdown.txt in step with the
README's changes; if upstream starts to use an AmigaOS call amigados.h does not have, the build says so (an
undeclared function): ports/common gets it.
